Malbolge program to print "नमस्ते दुनिया" (Hello, World! in Hindi)

Malbolge is considered one of the most difficult programming languages ever created.
It was designed by Ben Olmstead in 1998 to be nearly impossible to program in.
Programs are typically generated by specialized tools rather than hand-written.

Features that make Malbolge extremely difficult:
- Instructions are encrypted based on their position in memory
- The code modifies itself as it executes
- Only specific characters are valid instructions
- Memory addresses use base-3 (ternary) arithmetic
- The instruction set is intentionally obscure

UTF-8 byte sequences needed for "नमस्ते दुनिया":
  न = 224 164 168 (0xE0 0xA4 0xA8)
  म = 224 164 174 (0xE0 0xA4 0xAE)
  स = 224 165 141 (0xE0 0xA5 0x8D)
  ् = 224 165 141 (0xE0 0xA5 0x8D) [combining mark]
  ते = 224 164 164 224 165 135 (0xE0 0xA4 0xA4 0xE0 0xA5 0x87)
  (space) = 32 (0x20)
  दु = 224 164 166 224 165 129 (0xE0 0xA4 0xA6 0xE0 0xA5 0x81)
  नि = 224 164 168 224 164 191 (0xE0 0xA4 0xA8 0xE0 0xA4 0xBF)
  या = 224 164 175 224 164 190 (0xE0 0xA4 0xAF 0xE0 0xA4 0xBE)

Due to the extreme complexity of Malbolge and its self-modifying encrypted nature,
a working Malbolge program for UTF-8 Hindi output would require specialized generation tools.
The code below represents the structure but would need to be generated by a Malbolge compiler.

Malbolge instruction set:
  j = data pointer to *d
  i = C register from [d]
  * = rotate A register right
  p = output A as ASCII
  o = input ASCII to A
  / = memory to A
  v = data pointer from *d

